WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.278
Here is a bare DNA template. DNA polymerase cannot begin a complementary strand

00:00:04.278 --> 00:00:08.556
from scratch. It needs an existing complementary start with a three prime end

00:00:08.556 --> 00:00:12.833
that it can extend.

00:00:12.833 --> 00:00:17.806
Primase supplies that start. It builds a short RNA primer complementary to the

00:00:17.806 --> 00:00:22.778
template. Unlike DNA polymerase, primase can begin on a single stranded template without

00:00:22.778 --> 00:00:27.750
an existing complementary strand.

00:00:27.750 --> 00:00:32.972
Now DNA polymerase adds DNA nucleotides to the primer's three prime end. The

00:00:32.972 --> 00:00:38.194
strand grows five prime to three prime. Primase makes the start; polymerase extends

00:00:38.194 --> 00:00:43.417
it. The template by itself was not that start.
